zl1 sensors: the stack came back without a reboot -- 2026-09-23
=============================================================

One boot, uptime 41,183 s -> 41,914 s, no reboot, no partition write.


THE STATE IT STARTED IN
=======================
scripts/device/zl1-sensorfw-probe.sh --load-all --settle 12 --gap 8 --keep 45
at uptime 41,183 s. All nine plugins load (true,) and every sensor is STALE:

  local.AccelerometerSensor.xyz     timestamp 12323934179 (uptime 12323.9 s)
  local.GyroscopeSensor.value       timestamp 12323912176
  local.MagnetometerSensor.magneticField  timestamp 12323951238
  local.OrientationSensor.orientation (11902936926, 6)
  local.ALSSensor.lux               timestamp 0 -- never

Ages printed by the probe: accel 28,975 s, gyro 29,037 s, mag 29,068 s,
orientation 29,520 s, ALS 41,330 s (i.e. never).

THE DISCRIMINATOR: the ages are OLDER THAN THE RUNNING HAL
=========================================================
ps -eo pid,etimes at uptime 40,806 s:

  sensors@1.0-service  pid 938936  etimes 28437   -> started at uptime 12369 s
  sensorfwd            pid 216350  etimes 33007   -> started at uptime  7799 s
  sensors.qti          pid 41607   etimes 40758   -> since boot

The newest sample anywhere was uptime 12,323.9 s, which is 45 s BEFORE the
running sensors HAL was even started (12,369 s). So the HAL instance that was
alive had never delivered a single sample: its whole life was silent, and the
"last" values came from its predecessor. (That is the same argument the probe's
own footer makes -- compare the age against the uptime -- applied to a process
instead of to the daemon.)


WHAT THE KERNEL COULD SHOW: A STUCK HIDL CALL
=============================================
On this kernel /sys/kernel/debug/binder is ONE file holding both contexts --
"context binder" and "context hwbinder" per process -- so a HIDL call is
readable from the host. At uptime 40,982 s:

proc 938936                                  (the sensors HAL)
context hwbinder
  thread 938936: l 02 need_return 0 tr 0
    incoming transaction 1838445: ffffffc0ed8f6600 from 216350:939021 to 938936:938936 \
      code 4 flags 10 pri 0:120 r1 node 1838258 size 44:0 data ffffff800fd00000
  thread 939020: l 11 need_return 0 tr 0
  node 1838258: u0000007aca42d380 c0000007aca4411e0 pri 0:120 hs 1 hw 1 ls 1 lw 0 \
      is 2 iw 2 tr 1 proc 216350 40677
proc 216350                                  (sensorfwd)
context hwbinder
  thread 939021: l 10 need_return 0 tr 0
    outgoing transaction 1838445: ... from 216350:939021 to 938936:938936 code 4 flags 10 ... r1
  ref 1838283: desc 1 node 1838258 s 1 w 0 d 0000000000000000

  * node 1838258 is the ISensors node, held by exactly sensorfwd (216350) and
    hwservicemanager (40677).
  * code 4 is HIDL method index 4 = ISensors::batch(); r1 = need_reply = 1, so
    it is a two-way call. size 44 = 24 bytes of HIDL parcel padding + handle(4)
    + sampling period(8) + max report latency(8).
  * The client's thread 939021 sits in binder_thread_read -- waiting for the
    reply. The server's main thread 938936 has that transaction on its stack
    (i.e. it is INSIDE batch()) and its wchan is futex_wait_queue_me, so it is
    parked on a userspace lock inside the call, not in the kernel.
  * Re-read 6 s later: identical. The call is permanent, not slow.
  * Its age is consistent with the silence: the newest sample is 12,323.9 s and
    the stack has been mute for 28,900 s.

Rest of the HAL at the same moment (75 threads, all state S):
  37 futex_wait_queue_me | 36 poll_schedule_timeout | 1 diagchar_read | 1 binder_thread_read

The container's binder is otherwise silent -- sampling the highest transaction
id twice, 61 s apart:

  tx id t0=1993203 uptime=40982
  tx id t1=1993203 uptime=41043
  delta=0 over 61 s -> 0 tx/s

i.e. at that point nothing in the container was transacting at all, which is
why the stuck call could not be dated by the rate and was dated by the sample
ages instead.


STEP 1 -- KILL THE CONTAINER'S SENSORS HAL (init restarts it)
============================================================
uptime 41,260 s:  kill -9 938936
uptime 41,273 s:  new pid 1785710, comm sensors@1.0-ser, ppid 35057 (container
                  init), 74 threads, main thread idle in binder_thread_read
                  -- the wedge is gone (the transaction disappeared with the
                  process).
init's service name is vendor.sensors-hal-1-0 (class hal, no oneshot);
getprop init.svc.vendor.sensors-hal-1-0 -> running.
It re-registered: node 2174420, held by 40677 (hwservicemanager) ONLY.

That alone changed nothing: a probe at uptime ~41,300 s still reported every
sensor STALE with the SAME timestamps as before the kill (orientation still
11,902,936,926). The fresh HAL was healthy and talking to nobody -- no client
held a reference to its node.


STEP 2 -- RESTART THE CLIENT (systemctl restart sensorfwd)
=========================================================
uptime 41,575 s. Old sensorfwd 216350 -> new 1796648, ActiveState active,
NRestarts 0, and it did NOT take the HAL down with it: 1785710 was still the
HAL 60 s later (0 of 1 restarts killed it -- docs/ubuntu-touch/71 records 6/6,
see doc 78 section 4). repowerd (pid 1320231) was untouched throughout.

What the fresh daemon exported: only three bus objects exist after the restart
-- /SensorManager/alssensor, /SensorManager/magnetometersensor and
/SensorManager/orientationsensor, i.e. what repowerd subscribes to. Its
requestSensor session ids started at 11, not 58, confirming a fresh daemon.

Measurements after it, all by a NEW client pid (so not a leftover session):

  uptime 41,754 s probe (settle 20 / gap 12):
    local.MagnetometerSensor.magneticField
      read #1: (41807325000, 3, 14700, -15420, -44219, ...)   age 0 s
      read #2: (41819417006, 3, 14460, -16200, -43980, ...)   age 0 s
      => STREAMING
    local.ALSSensor.lux         (0, 96)  => STALE (timestamp 0: still never)
    local.OrientationSensor.orientation (41576823352, 6) => STALE, age 262 s

  uptime 41,878 s, accelerometer and gyroscope requested explicitly
