THE NINE SENSORS, ONE PAGE
==========================
Every one of them asked the same three things (loadPlugin, requestSensor with a held
session, then id/type/description/interval/isValid), plus a two-read value probe.
uptime ~42,400-42,900 s.

sensor                iface                      interval isValid  description
accelerometersensor   local.AccelerometerSensor  (100,)   (true,)  'x, y, and z axes accelerations in mG'
alssensor             local.ALSSensor            (0,)     (true,)  'ambient light intensity in lux'
compasssensor         local.CompassSensor        (100,)   (true,)  'compass north in degrees'
gyroscopesensor       local.GyroscopeSensor      (50,)    (true,)  'x, y, and z axes angular velocity in mdps'
magnetometersensor    local.MagnetometerSensor   (10,)    (true,)  'magnetic flux density in nT'
orientationsensor     local.OrientationSensor    (100,)   (true,)  'orientation of the device screen as 6 pre-defined positions'
pressuresensor        (no object)                --       --       --
proximitysensor       local.ProximitySensor      (0,)     (true,)  'whether an object is close to device screen'
rotationsensor        local.RotationSensor       (100,)   (true,)  'x, y, and z axes rotation in degrees'

values and verdicts, same run:

  accel  (42444636498, -3.1882, 20.2400, 1009.1403)        => STREAMING
  als    (0, 99) -> (0, 99)                                => STALE, timestamp 0 (never stamped)
  compass(42473268471, 225, 225, 225, 3)                   => STREAMING
  gyro   (42487687738, -92.672, -53.330, 8.743)            => STREAMING
  mag    (42502176902, 3, 14939, -15420, -43800, ...)      => STREAMING
  orient (41576823352, 6)                                  => STALE, age 947 s (by design, see below)
  pressure  (no object; loadPlugin returns true)
  prox   (42524902253, uint32 5)                           => SLOW (6 s old, 14 s at the 2nd read)
  rotation (42545211944, -1.0, 180.0, -45.0)               => STREAMING

Five sensors are continuous streams (accel, gyro, mag, compass, rotation); proximity is an
on-change sensor too (its value was 6 s old and did not move in the window -- "whether an
object is close to device screen", so a per-second rate was never the right expectation);
orientationsensor is the six-position classifier (below); alssensor streams without
stamping; pressure has no object.


THE ORIENTATION SENSOR IS A SIX-POSITION CLASSIFIER
===================================================
sensorfw's own introspection of /SensorManager/orientationsensor:

  description: 'orientation of the device screen as 6 pre-defined positions'
  type:        OrientationSensorChannel
  interval:    100 ms;  getAvailableIntervals [(5.0, 1000.0, 0.0)]

That is Android's DEVICE ORIENTATION (HIDL SENSOR_TYPE_DEVICE_ORIENTATION = 25): six
discrete positions (1 portrait, 2 landscape, 3 reverse portrait, 4 reverse landscape,
5 face up, 6 face down), not a stream of angles. A position classifier is on-change by
definition, so "no new sample while the phone sits still" is correct behaviour and the
probe's age test can never report anything but STALE for it. That is a limit of the
criterion, not a fault of the sensor. Docs 70/71/78 listed it as "does not produce";
that reading is withdrawn.

THE ORIENTATION WATCH
=====================
scripts/device/zl1-orientation-watch.sh --seconds 8 --interval 2, phone untouched
(uptime 42,595-42,602 s, display off):

   uptime    orientation  sample age        accelerometer (mG)
     42595   6            1018              -4.0719561576843262, 22.42921257019043, 1010.0505981445312
     42597   6            1020              -3.7063050270080566, 21.357154846191406, 1010.0505981445312
     42599   6            1023              -3.9552590847015381, 21.957756042480469, 1010.850341796875
     42602   6            1025              -3.062136173248291, 21.310476303100586, 1010.4893798828125
  positions seen: 6   changes: 0

The accelerometer z is steady at +1010 mG. In Android's frame (+Z out of the screen) a
phone lying face up reads z ~ +1000 mG, i.e. position 5 (face up) -- and the classifier
says 6 (face down). The two disagree. Which one is wrong needs a hand: pick the phone up,
turn it over, and watch both columns. If 6 becomes 5 when the screen faces down, the
classifier's numbering simply differs from AOSP's; if it stays 6, the classification
itself is stuck (note ro.vendor.sensors.dev_ori: false).

This matters beyond curiosity: qtmir reads this value to decide the shell's orientation,
and docs 71 measured 6 being mapped to Qt::InvertedLandscapeOrientation -- which is where
"the shell keeps going landscape" (docs 70/71) comes from.


